A slug is a URL-friendly, human-readable version of a name or title. It is commonly used in web applications to create clean, SEO-friendly URLs.

For example, instead of using:

https://shop.com/category?id=123

you use:

https://shop.com/categories/electronics

Here, electronics is the slug.

Why Do We Use Slugs?
1. SEO (Search Engine Optimization)

Search engines prefer meaningful URLs.

❌ Not SEO-friendly:

https://shop.com/category/15

✅ SEO-friendly:

https://shop.com/category/mobile-phones

Google can understand that the page is about mobile phones.

2. User-Friendly URLs

Compare these:

https://shop.com/category/7

vs.

https://shop.com/category/home-kitchen

The second URL is much easier for users to read, remember, and share.

3. Better Navigation

For an e-commerce website:

/categories/electronics

/categories/electronics/mobiles

/categories/electronics/mobiles/android

/products/iphone-16-pro-max

These URLs clearly describe the resource.

Examples
Category Name	Generated Slug
Electronics	electronics
Mobile Phones	mobile-phones
Home & Kitchen	home-kitchen
Men's Fashion	mens-fashion
Laptops & Accessories	laptops-accessories
How a Slug Is Generated

Suppose the category name is:

Home & Kitchen

Steps:

Convert to lowercase:
home & kitchen
Remove special characters:
home kitchen
Replace spaces with hyphens:
home-kitchen

Final slug:

home-kitchen
Handling Duplicate Slugs

Suppose you already have:

electronics

and another category with the same name is created.

Your SlugUtil can generate:

electronics-1
electronics-2
electronics-3

This ensures each URL remains unique.

Where You'll Use Slugs
Category
GET /api/v1/categories/electronics
Product
GET /api/v1/products/iphone-16-pro-max
Brand
GET /api/v1/brands/apple
Blog
GET /blog/spring-boot-security-guide
Database Example
id	name	slug
1	Electronics	electronics
2	Mobile Phones	mobile-phones
3	Home & Kitchen	home-kitchen

The slug is typically stored in the database and should have a unique constraint.

Spring Boot Example

If a user sends:

{
  "name": "Home & Kitchen"
}

Your SlugUtil generates:

String slug = slugUtil.generateUniqueSlug(request.getName());

category.setSlug(slug);

The database stores:

name : Home & Kitchen

slug : home-kitchen
Enterprise Best Practice

In production e-commerce platforms, slugs should be generated automatically by the backend, not provided by the client. This ensures:

Consistent URL formatting.
Uniqueness across resources.
Protection against invalid or malicious input.
SEO-friendly URLs without relying on client-side logic.